{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You have a Node.js backend and want to add AI capabilities. By the end of this guide, you'll have a working chat endpoint powered by TanStack AI and OpenAI -- no UI framework required."}]},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tip:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" If you'd prefer not to sign up with individual AI providers, "},{"type":"link","href":"../adapters/openrouter","children":[{"type":"text","value":"OpenRouter"}]},{"type":"text","value":" gives you access to 300+ models with a single API key and is the easiest way to get started."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"installation","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Installation"}]},{"type":"component","name":"tabs","attributes":{"variant":"package-manager","mode":"install"},"children":[],"properties":{"data-package-manager-meta":"{\"packagesByFramework\":{\"react\":[[\"@tanstack/ai\",\"@tanstack/ai-openai\"]],\"vue\":[[\"@tanstack/ai\",\"@tanstack/ai-openai\"]],\"solid\":[[\"@tanstack/ai\",\"@tanstack/ai-openai\"]],\"svelte\":[[\"@tanstack/ai\",\"@tanstack/ai-openai\"]],\"preact\":[[\"@tanstack/ai\",\"@tanstack/ai-openai\"]],\"angular\":[[\"@tanstack/ai\",\"@tanstack/ai-openai\"]],\"vanilla\":[[\"@tanstack/ai\",\"@tanstack/ai-openai\"]],\"octane\":[[\"@tanstack/ai\",\"@tanstack/ai-openai\"]]},\"mode\":\"install\"}"}},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"basic-chat","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Basic Chat"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The simplest way to get a response -- call "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"},{"type":"text","value":" and collect the text:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { chat, streamToText } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\n\nconst stream = chat({\n  adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n  messages: [{ role: 'user', content: 'Hello!' }],\n})\n\nconst { text } = await streamToText(stream)\nconsole.log(text)","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"},{"type":"text","value":" returns an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"AsyncIterable<StreamChunk>"},{"type":"text","value":". "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"streamToText"},{"type":"text","value":" reads it to the end and returns "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"{ text, chunks }"},{"type":"text","value":". "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"text"},{"type":"text","value":" is the full reply. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chunks"},{"type":"text","value":" holds the rest of the run: tool calls, usage, and interrupts."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"http-endpoint","children":[{"type":"text","value":"HTTP Endpoint"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Here's an Express server that exposes a streaming chat endpoint using Server-Sent Events:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import express from 'express'\nimport { chat, toServerSentEventsResponse } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\n\nconst app = express()\napp.use(express.json())\n\napp.post('/api/chat', async (req, res) => {\n  const { messages } = req.body\n\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n    messages,\n  })\n\n  const response = toServerSentEventsResponse(stream)\n  res.writeHead(response.status, Object.fromEntries(response.headers))\n\n  const body = response.body\n  if (body) {\n    const reader = body.getReader()\n    const pump = async () => {\n      const { done, value } = await reader.read()\n      if (done) {\n        res.end()\n        return\n      }\n      res.write(value)\n      await pump()\n    }\n    await pump()\n  }\n})\n\napp.listen(3000, () => console.log('Server running on port 3000'))","lang":"typescript","meta":"ignore"},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tip:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" Any backend that returns the TanStack AI SSE format works -- you can use Fastify, Hono, or any other Node.js framework."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This endpoint is compatible with TanStack AI's client-side "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useChat"},{"type":"text","value":" hooks ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-react"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-vue"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-svelte"},{"type":"text","value":"), so you can pair it with any frontend later."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"with-tools","children":[{"type":"text","value":"With Tools"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Define a server tool with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"toolDefinition"},{"type":"text","value":" and pass it to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"},{"type":"text","value":". The agent loop automatically calls your tool and feeds the result back to the model:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { chat, toolDefinition, streamToText } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\n\nconst getWeather = toolDefinition({\n  name: 'getWeather',\n  description: 'Get weather for a city',\n  inputSchema: z.object({ city: z.string() }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({ temp: z.number(), condition: z.string() }),\n}).server(async ({ city }) => {\n  return { temp: 22, condition: 'sunny' }\n})\n\nconst stream = chat({\n  adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n  messages: [{ role: 'user', content: 'Weather in Tokyo?' }],\n  tools: [getWeather],\n})\n\nconst { text } = await streamToText(stream)\nconsole.log(text)","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The model decides when to call "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getWeather"},{"type":"text","value":", receives the result, and incorporates it into its response -- all within a single "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"},{"type":"text","value":" call."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"alternative-response-formats","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Alternative Response Formats"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack AI ships several ways to return a stream over HTTP:"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"toHttpResponse()"}]},{"type":"text","value":" returns a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Response"},{"type":"text","value":" using newline-delimited JSON instead of SSE. Pair it with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fetchHttpStream"},{"type":"text","value":" on the client:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { chat, toHttpResponse } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const { messages } = await request.json()\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n    messages,\n  })\n  return toHttpResponse(stream)\n}","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Raw stream consumption"}]},{"type":"text","value":" -- iterate the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"AsyncIterable"},{"type":"text","value":" directly with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"for await"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { stream } from './stream'\n\nfor await (const chunk of stream) {\n  if (chunk.type === 'TEXT_MESSAGE_CONTENT') {\n    process.stdout.write(chunk.delta ?? '')\n  }\n}","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This gives you full control over every chunk type (text deltas, tool calls, run lifecycle events, etc.)."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"environment-variables","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Environment Variables"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Create a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":".env"},{"type":"text","value":" file with your API key:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"# OpenRouter (recommended — access 300+ models with one key)\nOPENROUTER_API_KEY=sk-or-...\n\n# OpenAI\nOPENAI_API_KEY=your-openai-api-key","lang":"bash"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The adapter reads "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"OPENAI_API_KEY"},{"type":"text","value":" at runtime. Never expose it to the browser."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"next-steps","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Next Steps"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Learn about "},{"type":"link","href":"../tools/tools","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tools"}]},{"type":"text","value":" to add function calling and agent loops"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Explore "},{"type":"link","href":"../reference/classes/StreamProcessor","children":[{"type":"text","value":"StreamProcessor"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for fine-grained stream control"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Check out the "},{"type":"link","href":"../adapters/openai","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Adapters"}]},{"type":"text","value":" to connect to different providers"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"See the "},{"type":"link","href":"./quick-start","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Quick Start"}]},{"type":"text","value":" to add a frontend"}]}]}]}],"headings":[{"id":"installation","text":"Installation","level":2},{"id":"basic-chat","text":"Basic Chat","level":2},{"id":"http-endpoint","text":"HTTP Endpoint","level":2},{"id":"with-tools","text":"With Tools","level":2},{"id":"alternative-response-formats","text":"Alternative Response Formats","level":2},{"id":"environment-variables","text":"Environment Variables","level":2},{"id":"next-steps","text":"Next Steps","level":2}]},"frameworks":["react","vue","solid","svelte","preact","angular","vanilla","octane"],"downloadMarkdown":"\nYou have a Node.js backend and want to add AI capabilities. By the end of this guide, you'll have a working chat endpoint powered by TanStack AI and OpenAI -- no UI framework required.\n\n> **Tip:** If you'd prefer not to sign up with individual AI providers, [OpenRouter](../adapters/openrouter) gives you access to 300+ models with a single API key and is the easiest way to get started.\n\n## Installation\n\n<!-- ::start:tabs variant=\"package-manager\" mode=\"install\" -->\n\nreact: @tanstack/ai @tanstack/ai-openai\nvue: @tanstack/ai @tanstack/ai-openai\nsolid: @tanstack/ai @tanstack/ai-openai\nsvelte: @tanstack/ai @tanstack/ai-openai\npreact: @tanstack/ai @tanstack/ai-openai\nangular: @tanstack/ai @tanstack/ai-openai\nvanilla: @tanstack/ai @tanstack/ai-openai\noctane: @tanstack/ai @tanstack/ai-openai\n\n<!-- ::end:tabs -->\n\n## Basic Chat\n\nThe simplest way to get a response -- call `chat()` and collect the text:\n\n```typescript\nimport { chat, streamToText } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\n\nconst stream = chat({\n  adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n  messages: [{ role: 'user', content: 'Hello!' }],\n})\n\nconst { text } = await streamToText(stream)\nconsole.log(text)\n```\n\n`chat()` returns an `AsyncIterable<StreamChunk>`. `streamToText` reads it to the end and returns `{ text, chunks }`. `text` is the full reply. `chunks` holds the rest of the run: tool calls, usage, and interrupts.\n\n## HTTP Endpoint\n\nHere's an Express server that exposes a streaming chat endpoint using Server-Sent Events:\n\n```typescript ignore\nimport express from 'express'\nimport { chat, toServerSentEventsResponse } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\n\nconst app = express()\napp.use(express.json())\n\napp.post('/api/chat', async (req, res) => {\n  const { messages } = req.body\n\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n    messages,\n  })\n\n  const response = toServerSentEventsResponse(stream)\n  res.writeHead(response.status, Object.fromEntries(response.headers))\n\n  const body = response.body\n  if (body) {\n    const reader = body.getReader()\n    const pump = async () => {\n      const { done, value } = await reader.read()\n      if (done) {\n        res.end()\n        return\n      }\n      res.write(value)\n      await pump()\n    }\n    await pump()\n  }\n})\n\napp.listen(3000, () => console.log('Server running on port 3000'))\n```\n\n> **Tip:** Any backend that returns the TanStack AI SSE format works -- you can use Fastify, Hono, or any other Node.js framework.\n\nThis endpoint is compatible with TanStack AI's client-side `useChat` hooks (`@tanstack/ai-react`, `@tanstack/ai-vue`, `@tanstack/ai-svelte`), so you can pair it with any frontend later.\n\n## With Tools\n\nDefine a server tool with `toolDefinition` and pass it to `chat()`. The agent loop automatically calls your tool and feeds the result back to the model:\n\n```typescript\nimport { chat, toolDefinition, streamToText } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\n\nconst getWeather = toolDefinition({\n  name: 'getWeather',\n  description: 'Get weather for a city',\n  inputSchema: z.object({ city: z.string() }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({ temp: z.number(), condition: z.string() }),\n}).server(async ({ city }) => {\n  return { temp: 22, condition: 'sunny' }\n})\n\nconst stream = chat({\n  adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n  messages: [{ role: 'user', content: 'Weather in Tokyo?' }],\n  tools: [getWeather],\n})\n\nconst { text } = await streamToText(stream)\nconsole.log(text)\n```\n\nThe model decides when to call `getWeather`, receives the result, and incorporates it into its response -- all within a single `chat()` call.\n\n## Alternative Response Formats\n\nTanStack AI ships several ways to return a stream over HTTP:\n\n**`toHttpResponse()`** returns a `Response` using newline-delimited JSON instead of SSE. Pair it with `fetchHttpStream` on the client:\n\n```typescript\nimport { chat, toHttpResponse } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const { messages } = await request.json()\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n    messages,\n  })\n  return toHttpResponse(stream)\n}\n```\n\n**Raw stream consumption** -- iterate the `AsyncIterable` directly with `for await`:\n\n```typescript\nimport { stream } from './stream'\n\nfor await (const chunk of stream) {\n  if (chunk.type === 'TEXT_MESSAGE_CONTENT') {\n    process.stdout.write(chunk.delta ?? '')\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThis gives you full control over every chunk type (text deltas, tool calls, run lifecycle events, etc.).\n\n## Environment Variables\n\nCreate a `.env` file with your API key:\n\n```bash\n# OpenRouter (recommended — access 300+ models with one key)\nOPENROUTER_API_KEY=sk-or-...\n\n# OpenAI\nOPENAI_API_KEY=your-openai-api-key\n```\n\nThe adapter reads `OPENAI_API_KEY` at runtime. Never expose it to the browser.\n\n## Next Steps\n\n- Learn about [Tools](../tools/tools) to add function calling and agent loops\n- Explore [StreamProcessor](../reference/classes/StreamProcessor) for fine-grained stream control\n- Check out the [Adapters](../adapters/openai) to connect to different providers\n- See the [Quick Start](./quick-start) to add a frontend\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: \"Quick Start: Server Only\"\nid: quick-start-server\norder: 5\ndescription: \"Add a streaming AI chat endpoint to a Node.js backend with TanStack AI — no UI framework required.\"\nkeywords:\n  - tanstack ai\n  - node.js\n  - server\n  - backend\n  - quick start\n  - streaming chat\n  - openai\n  - sse\n---\n\nYou have a Node.js backend and want to add AI capabilities. By the end of this guide, you'll have a working chat endpoint powered by TanStack AI and OpenAI -- no UI framework required.\n\n> **Tip:** If you'd prefer not to sign up with individual AI providers, [OpenRouter](../adapters/openrouter) gives you access to 300+ models with a single API key and is the easiest way to get started.\n\n## Installation\n\n<!-- ::start:tabs variant=\"package-manager\" mode=\"install\" -->\n\nreact: @tanstack/ai @tanstack/ai-openai\nvue: @tanstack/ai @tanstack/ai-openai\nsolid: @tanstack/ai @tanstack/ai-openai\nsvelte: @tanstack/ai @tanstack/ai-openai\npreact: @tanstack/ai @tanstack/ai-openai\nangular: @tanstack/ai @tanstack/ai-openai\nvanilla: @tanstack/ai @tanstack/ai-openai\noctane: @tanstack/ai @tanstack/ai-openai\n\n<!-- ::end:tabs -->\n\n## Basic Chat\n\nThe simplest way to get a response -- call `chat()` and collect the text:\n\n```typescript\nimport { chat, streamToText } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\n\nconst stream = chat({\n  adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n  messages: [{ role: 'user', content: 'Hello!' }],\n})\n\nconst { text } = await streamToText(stream)\nconsole.log(text)\n```\n\n`chat()` returns an `AsyncIterable<StreamChunk>`. `streamToText` reads it to the end and returns `{ text, chunks }`. `text` is the full reply. `chunks` holds the rest of the run: tool calls, usage, and interrupts.\n\n## HTTP Endpoint\n\nHere's an Express server that exposes a streaming chat endpoint using Server-Sent Events:\n\n```typescript ignore\nimport express from 'express'\nimport { chat, toServerSentEventsResponse } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\n\nconst app = express()\napp.use(express.json())\n\napp.post('/api/chat', async (req, res) => {\n  const { messages } = req.body\n\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n    messages,\n  })\n\n  const response = toServerSentEventsResponse(stream)\n  res.writeHead(response.status, Object.fromEntries(response.headers))\n\n  const body = response.body\n  if (body) {\n    const reader = body.getReader()\n    const pump = async () => {\n      const { done, value } = await reader.read()\n      if (done) {\n        res.end()\n        return\n      }\n      res.write(value)\n      await pump()\n    }\n    await pump()\n  }\n})\n\napp.listen(3000, () => console.log('Server running on port 3000'))\n```\n\n> **Tip:** Any backend that returns the TanStack AI SSE format works -- you can use Fastify, Hono, or any other Node.js framework.\n\nThis endpoint is compatible with TanStack AI's client-side `useChat` hooks (`@tanstack/ai-react`, `@tanstack/ai-vue`, `@tanstack/ai-svelte`), so you can pair it with any frontend later.\n\n## With Tools\n\nDefine a server tool with `toolDefinition` and pass it to `chat()`. The agent loop automatically calls your tool and feeds the result back to the model:\n\n```typescript\nimport { chat, toolDefinition, streamToText } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\n\nconst getWeather = toolDefinition({\n  name: 'getWeather',\n  description: 'Get weather for a city',\n  inputSchema: z.object({ city: z.string() }),\n  outputSchema: z.object({ temp: z.number(), condition: z.string() }),\n}).server(async ({ city }) => {\n  return { temp: 22, condition: 'sunny' }\n})\n\nconst stream = chat({\n  adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n  messages: [{ role: 'user', content: 'Weather in Tokyo?' }],\n  tools: [getWeather],\n})\n\nconst { text } = await streamToText(stream)\nconsole.log(text)\n```\n\nThe model decides when to call `getWeather`, receives the result, and incorporates it into its response -- all within a single `chat()` call.\n\n## Alternative Response Formats\n\nTanStack AI ships several ways to return a stream over HTTP:\n\n**`toHttpResponse()`** returns a `Response` using newline-delimited JSON instead of SSE. Pair it with `fetchHttpStream` on the client:\n\n```typescript\nimport { chat, toHttpResponse } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const { messages } = await request.json()\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n    messages,\n  })\n  return toHttpResponse(stream)\n}\n```\n\n**Raw stream consumption** -- iterate the `AsyncIterable` directly with `for await`:\n\n```typescript\nimport { stream } from './stream'\n\nfor await (const chunk of stream) {\n  if (chunk.type === 'TEXT_MESSAGE_CONTENT') {\n    process.stdout.write(chunk.delta ?? '')\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThis gives you full control over every chunk type (text deltas, tool calls, run lifecycle events, etc.).\n\n## Environment Variables\n\nCreate a `.env` file with your API key:\n\n```bash\n# OpenRouter (recommended — access 300+ models with one key)\nOPENROUTER_API_KEY=sk-or-...\n\n# OpenAI\nOPENAI_API_KEY=your-openai-api-key\n```\n\nThe adapter reads `OPENAI_API_KEY` at runtime. Never expose it to the browser.\n\n## Next Steps\n\n- Learn about [Tools](../tools/tools) to add function calling and agent loops\n- Explore [StreamProcessor](../reference/classes/StreamProcessor) for fine-grained stream control\n- Check out the [Adapters](../adapters/openai) to connect to different providers\n- See the [Quick Start](./quick-start) to add a frontend\n"}
